now  who collected these things and put them  together and edited them how much  editing they did  when they were  first started to circulate  around the 4th century we start seeing  references to them in other authors so  it's clear that they had been  been published by then but the first  couple of centuries are kind of kind of  a blank  beyond that  like all ancient texts they were copied  and recopied  by generations of of scribes  and um the meditations is actually one  of those texts that very nearly didn't  survive  it drops out of sight for hundreds of  years at a time we hear you know a brief  mention of it at one point in the 9th  century it pops up again in the 12th  century  and  [Music]  the  it's one of those cases where  the first printed edition  is actually  the  best source for the text of of the  meditations it was obviously based on um  a handwritten manuscript which hasn't  survived  but  it's a very very tenuous kind of of thin  line that has brought the meditations to  us  um and as a result there are a lot of  places in the text where it's very  difficult to figure out um what's going  on there are clearly copyists errors um  you know entries that have gotten  squished together or that need to be  separated  it's a very messy text to try and make  sense of in some places i mean i've  tried to indicate that at points in the  translation where there's real doubt  about about what marcus is saying that  printed text that was in latin  no it was in greek  first book he quotes from female family  members is that uncommon  um  not so much in a roman context  um i mean there were a lot of of um  fairly dynamic upper class roman women  running around and i don't i don't find  it at all surprising that some of  marcus's family members were were in  that category um  i mean in general the family members get  much less play than than people later on  teachers and mentors and so forth um  mostly for you know grandfather  grandmother mother it's just sort of one  or two lines  um  but the women are certainly there  given the earlier philosophy that dealt  so much with the civic aspect this seems  like almost a a retreat or a  disengagement from that  is that how you would take it  um  i don't think so i think it it appears  that way to some extent because it's  marcus talking to himself dealing with  his own problems  um if he'd been writing for a wider  audience i think he probably would have  said more about  you know the way that people should  behave in society  how society should be should be  structured perhaps  those sorts of things but this is really  a sort of private talking to oneself  and i think that's  that's what's responsible for the kind  of  um  almost self-absorption that that one  feels sometimes  how do you think 21st century america  would i mean reacts to a philosophy like  this and in a related question who  besides john ashcroft is getting a  complimentary a copy of your book  um well to answer your last question  first um you know our previous president  has cited the meditations as  his favorite book and one that he  rereads every few years i haven't  actually sent him a copy perhaps i  should  [Music]  in terms of of how 21st century  americans would respond to it i don't  know i suppose they would respond to it  in different ways  i think that that there are a lot of  things  in the meditations that  [Music]  that people would find very sympathetic  today as they have it you know for the  past few centuries  um  a lot of the things that people seem to  be concerned about today achieving inner  serenity um handling stress those sorts  of things are problems that marcus is  facing  there are other aspects of of the  meditations that i think we probably  find it harder to access  i mean scientific knowledge has  obviously moved on since the stoics  you know there are certain aspects of  stoicism as a comprehensive philosophy  that nobody could accept today  um  so it's you know like most ancient  literature it's a mixed bag  um but i think that that  that's one of the things that that  drives us to read ancient literature is  to ask you know what here is familiar  what here is novel or strange to us  and to compare back and forth  did you run into any suggestion that  this manuscript was preserved and  protected by the early christians  um i think that's unlikely um i mean  marcus himself  was the titular head of a government  that was  suppressing christians often rather  violently  there are one or two cases in the  meditations where  people have suspected that there might  be a kind of covert reference to the  christians  i'm inclined myself to think not at  least in most cases i think the the  christians for marcus aurelius were  this you know small troublesome sect  um  who you know had to be dealt with  when they got out of hand and he had  officials to do that  i mean it's i think there's a good deal  in the meditations that is not  incompatible with  ancient christianity or many many modern  forms of it  but  you know marcus really certainly was not  a a crypto christian and i doubt that  most early christians would have found  him a very sympathetic figure he was for  them in many ways the enemy  does that  well i'm just thinking of the emperor  constantine and  they would probably have had access  to these manuscripts however they were  written  could they have been responsible for  protecting them  would they have seen these meditations  as a  philosophy related to their own beliefs  well by they you mean  the early christians under the emperor  um  [Music]  anything is possible  i mean the the early history of this  text is just so  blank to us  that you know i'm reluctant to say no  that couldn't have happened  it doesn't seem to me as likely as that  it was um  you know kept by  marcus aurelius's immediate successors  um you know a secretary even his son  commodus  could have tidied it up and put it into  circulation  but you know this is all guesswork  do we have the public voice of marcus  aurelius against which you could play  off  this private meditative voice well we do  to some extent i mean we have um  you know various  legal decisions and um you know letters  issued by marcus aurelius or in his name  in response to to embassies and appeals  and what have you um  it's kind of hard to draw a direct  connection there simply because  so much of marcus aurelius's public  voice may not really be marcus aurelius  at all it may be you know the  undersecretary for whatever um  responding to this particular petition  from the people of ephesus or what have  you  um  so it's it's hard to  to really compare the two i think  claire  i'm wondering about your experience as a  translator  and i have um two questions  related to that i wonder how you came to  translate this book and second if in the  process of translating  it became a way of meditative practice  for you and this comes from  the readings that i'm doing in buddhism  and as you mentioned  um the real connection  that once that i heard between the  buddhist thoughts and the the meditation  so i wonder and buddhism being very  anchored in the practice of everyday  life how you end it don't talk about  being a good man just be it  i wonder if  translating the daily practice it became  a certain practice where he was  addressed  okay um  to answer your first question  um i came to translate the meditations  almost by accident um will murphy my  editor at random house um emailed me out  of the blue one day and and said um do  you want to do you want to translate  something for us um and i said sure  maybe  and um we batted a few ideas around and  um i would say you know what about  roswitha the well-known medieval nun who  wrote latin dramas and he said great  sounds like a terrific idea how about  marcus aurelius  and i would say what about philosophus's  life of apollonius and he would say wow  terrific how about marcus aurelius  and  after a while that became began to seem  the thing to do um  and it was it was an interesting project  for me because it's a little earlier  than most of my own scholarly work  and i'm really a latinist rather than  somebody who works on the greek side so  it was a chance for me to to explore  some new territory or at least sort of  new territory  and that was very enjoyable  um  as far as integrating marcus aurelius  into my own meditative practices um  i suppose it was meditative in a sort of  way i mean i did most of the translating  in various coffee shops around  charlottesville um  with you know a text of marcus aurelius  on the table on a stack of index cards  and so forth  it was  i mean it was certainly something that i  enjoyed translating and something that  was very helpful to me at that stage i  had been coming out of a bad bout of  depression  and the meditations um  you know is very helpful  in that kind of situation it's a very  calming  kind of work it makes you feel more  serene and more accepting about life  um and that i think was was a good thing  i'm certainly grateful to have to have  had that chance  no other questions  can you say a little bit about how this  translation differs for  differs from or is continuous with  previous translations  um sure the um  i mean the history of translating marcus  aurelius is is much richer in some ways  than a lot of other ancient texts it's  never been a text that that professional  classicists have been very interested in  are very exciting excited about um i  think because it's not  you know at all autobiographical  if we had you know marcus aurelius's  diary where he says you know woke up at  9am executed some christians went out  and had lunch whatever um that would be  really interesting um  so it's it's a text that that you know  is no good to social historians because  it's all about philosophy um it's also  not really a lot of use to students of  ancient philosophy because it's  you know fairly derivative in in purely  philosophical terms um you know marcus  aurelius wasn't  writing original philosophical thought  nor was he setting out to do that  um on the other hand it's always had a a  great appeal to  to non-classicist to the you know the  famous common reader  and so there have been a lot of of  english translations although curiously  none for for 30 or 35 years  in terms of what i was trying to do with  the translation  a lot of the earlier translations i  think make marcus aurelius sound much  more  formal  and much more um  almost king jamesy in some sense um than  i think the the greek text itself really  is um i mean you've got the sense that  this is marcus aurelius the great sage  um  you know offering you timeless wisdom to  guide your life by and i think that's  not really what the meditations is  so i was looking for a more  sort of straightforward  almost colloquial tone in some parts  that would really  make the translation  you know accessible to to people in the  21st century  okay well let me thank you all again for  for joining me on this occasion  thank you very much  thank you so  [Applause]  i'd like to invite much  up to speak further with greg and get a  signed copy of the meditations thank you  all for coming  meditations of marcus aurelius is  published by modern library a division  of random house  visit randomhouse.com  modern library for more information  [Music]

